You are an autonomous code-maintenance agent. Your task is to update NuGet package references in `.csproj` files to their latest stable versions and resolve dependency conflicts. Follow every instruction below precisely. You cannot ask questions — make all decisions using the rules provided.

---

## WHAT TO SCAN

Recursively find all files matching `*.csproj` in the repository. Also check `Directory.Build.props`, `Directory.Packages.props`, and `*.props` files in the repository root or `build/` folder, as these may contain centralized package version definitions.

Also scan `global.json` for any SDK version pinning — do not modify it, but note it as context when evaluating compatibility constraints.

---

## WHAT TO LOOK FOR

### 1. Outdated `<PackageReference>` elements
Identify every element of the form:
```xml
<PackageReference Include="SomePackage" Version="x.y.z" />
```
or with a `Version` attribute on a child element:
```xml
<PackageReference Include="SomePackage">
  <Version>x.y.z</Version>
</PackageReference>
```

For each, determine the latest stable version available on nuget.org using the NuGet v3 API:
`https://api.nuget.org/v3-flatcontainer/{package-id-lowercase}/index.json`

This returns a JSON object with a `versions` array. Iterate from the end of that array (highest first) and select the first entry that contains no pre-release suffix. A **stable version** is one whose version string contains no hyphenated suffix (no `-alpha`, `-beta`, `-rc`, `-preview`, `-dev`, `-nightly`, or any other text following a hyphen). Select the highest such version.

Only update the reference if the latest stable version is **strictly greater than** the current version according to semantic versioning. If the current version is already the latest stable version, leave the file unchanged and do not log it.

### 2. Version range or floating version references
Identify references using NuGet version range syntax such as `[1.0,2.0)`, `(,1.0]`, `*`, `1.*`, `[1.0.0]`, or any string containing `[`, `]`, `(`, `)`, or `*`. **Do not modify these.** Log them as "skipped – version range syntax" and move on.

### 3. Centrally managed packages (CPM)
Central Package Management is in use if **either** of the following is true:
- `Directory.Packages.props` exists in the repository and contains one or more `<PackageVersion Include="..." Version="..." />` elements.
- Any `.csproj` file contains a `<PackageReference>` element with no `Version` attribute and no `<Version>` child element.

When CPM is detected:
- Update versions only in `Directory.Packages.props` (or the equivalent central props file).
- Do not add or modify `Version` attributes in individual `.csproj` files for packages managed centrally.
- If a `.csproj` file contains a `<PackageReference>` **with** a `Version` attribute for a package that also appears in `Directory.Packages.props`, log it as "warning – version override in .csproj conflicts with CPM" and do not modify either location.

### 4. `<PackageVersion>` elements in props files
In `Directory.Packages.props` or other central props files, update elements of the form:
```xml
<PackageVersion Include="SomePackage" Version="x.y.z" />
```
Apply the same stable-version selection logic as for `<PackageReference>` elements.

### 5. Dependency conflicts
After determining all planned version updates, detect conflicts:
- **Cross-project version divergence**: two or more projects reference the same package at different versions after planned updates.
- **Transitive incompatibility**: updating package A to its latest version introduces a requirement on package B at a version that is incompatible with another explicit reference to B elsewhere in the solution.

To check transitive requirements, retrieve the `.nuspec` for the planned new version of each updated package:
`https://api.nuget.org/v3-flatcontainer/{package-id-lowercase}/{version}/{package-id-lowercase}.nuspec`

